WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.909
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:08.920
If we have an animation movie or a computer
game with quadrupeds, and we are yearning

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:14.980
for really high-quality, lifelike animations,
motion capture is often the go-to tool for

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:15.980
the job.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.920
Motion capture means that we put an actor,
in our case, a dog in the studio, we ask it

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:27.110
to perform sitting, trotting, pacing and jumping,
record its motion, and transfer it onto our

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.630
virtual character.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:33.730
In an earlier work, a learning-based technique
was introduced by the name Mode-Adaptive Neural

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:39.500
Network, and it was able to correctly weave
together these previously recorded motions,

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.410
and not only that, but it also addressed these
unnatural sliding motions that were produced

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:51.570
by previous works.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.720
As you see here, it also worked well on more
challenging landscapes.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.940
We talked about this paper approximately a
hundred videos, or in other words, a little

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:06.640
more than a year ago, and I noted that it
was scientifically interesting, it was evaluated

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.000
well, it had all the ingredients for a truly
excellent paper.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.560
But one thing was missing.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.690
So what is that one thing?

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:20.620
Well, we haven’t seen the characters interacting
with the scene itself.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:25.400
If you liked this previous paper, you are
going to be elated by this one because this

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:30.400
new work is from the very same group, and
goes by the name Neural State Machine, and

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.200
introduces character-scene interactions for
bipeds.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:40.000
Now, we suddenly jumped from a quadruped paper
to a biped one, and the reason for this is

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.229
because I was looking to introduce the concept
of foot sliding, which will be measured later

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:47.090
for this new method too.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.090
Stay tuned!

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:52.740
So, in this new problem formulation, we need
to guide the character to a challenging end

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:58.119
state, for instance, sitting in a chair, while
being able to maneuver through all kinds of

00:01:58.119 --> 00:01:59.119
geometry.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:04.040
We’ll use the chair example a fair bit in
the next minute or two, so I’ll stress that

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.489
this can do a whole lot more, the chair is
just used as a vehicle to get a taste of how

00:02:09.489 --> 00:02:10.819
this technique works.

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:14.720
But the end state needn’t just be some kind
of chair.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.549
It can be any chair!

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:20.579
This chair may have all kinds of different
heights and shapes, and the agent has to be

00:02:20.579 --> 00:02:26.549
able to change the animations and stitch them
together correctly regardless of the geometry.

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:31.969
To achieve this, the authors propose an interesting
new data augmentation model.

00:02:31.969 --> 00:02:35.989
Since we are working with neural networks,
we already have a training set to teach it

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:41.430
about motion, and data augmentation means
that we extend this dataset with lots and

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:47.549
lots of new information to make the AI generalize
better to unseen, real-world examples.

00:02:47.549 --> 00:02:50.249
So, how is this done here exactly?

00:02:50.249 --> 00:02:54.109
Well, the authors proposed a clever idea to
do this.

00:02:54.109 --> 00:02:57.049
Let’s walk through their five prescribed
steps.

00:02:57.049 --> 00:03:03.230
One, let’s use motion capture data, have
the subject sit down and see what the contact

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.260
points are when it happens.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:11.329
Two, we then record the curves that describe
the entirety of the motion of sitting down.

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:16.549
So far so good, but we are not interested
in one kind of chair, we want it to sit into

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:21.999
all kinds of chairs, so three, generate a
large selection of different geometries and

00:03:21.999 --> 00:03:25.449
adjust the location of these contact points
accordingly.

00:03:25.449 --> 00:03:31.949
Four, change the motion curves so they indeed
end at the new, transformed contact points.

00:03:31.949 --> 00:03:37.470
And five, move the joints of the character
to make it follow this motion curve and compute

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.019
the evolution of the character pose.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:45.370
We then pair up this motion with the chair
geometry and chuck it into the new, augmented

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:46.620
training set.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:51.980
Now, make no mistake, the paper contains much,
much more than this, so make sure to have

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.120
a look in the video description.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.819
So what do we get for all this work?

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:03.049
Well, have a look at this trembly character
from a previous paper, and look at the new

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:04.989
synthesized motions.

00:04:04.989 --> 00:04:09.290
Natural, smooth, creamy, and I don’t see
artifacts.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:15.330
Also, here you see some results that measure
the amount of foot sliding during these animations,

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.770
which is subject to minimization.

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:21.000
That means that the smaller the bars are,
the better.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:27.260
With NSM, you see how this Neural State Machine
method produces much less than previous methods,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:32.230
and now we see how cool it is that we talked
about the quadruped paper as well, because

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:39.030
we see that it even beats the MANN, the mode-adaptive
neural networks from the previous paper.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:44.040
That one had very little foot sliding, and
apparently, it can still be improved by quite

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.040
a bit.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.500
The positional and rotational errors in the
animation it offers are also by far the lowest

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:51.800
of the bunch.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.710
Since it works in real time, it can also be
used for computer games and virtual reality

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:57.980
applications.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:01.760
And all this improvement within a year of
work.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.270
What a time to be alive!

